  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced labour migration governance towards better protection and empowerment for Indonesian women migrant workers, domestic workers and fishers</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indonesia has become a significant migrant worker sending over the last four decades. The contribution of labour migration to the employment and economic conditions of Indonesia is important. Migrant workers, domestic workers, and capture fisheries workers often face significant risks in their jobs, including human rights violations, exploitation, and poor working conditions. Strengthening protections for these groups is essential to ensure they can enjoy basic rights, work under decent conditions, and are safeguarded against harmful practices. 

The International Labour Organization (ILO) and labour stakeholders in Indonesia recognize that all three groups are vulnerable to exploitation. They frequently endure poor working environments and are victims of human rights violations, including violence and harassment. Many find themselves trapped in cycles of forced labour and lack proper legal protection. 

In strengthening safe migration, activities over the next two years will focus on end-to-end protection across the migration cycle, from early-stage literacy, recruitment, contract awareness, and the strengthening of regular migration pathways at the village level, to protection during employment abroad. This will include enhanced transparency in recruitment processes and the monitoring of recruitment-related costs. In parallel, economic reintegration programmes for returning migrant workers will be further strengthened to support local economic development, particularly in rural areas.

The safe migration strengthening programme will also strengthen ILO&apos;s facilitation efforts to support Indonesia in preparing for the application of ILO Convention No. 188 on Work in Fishing. Through this facilitation, fishers working as migrant workers will have the assurance that they are consistently protected by the Government through a comprehensive regulatory framework, including robust enforcement mechanisms.</narrative>
  </description>
